Raw dev notes -- Keeping the Knowledge Flowing (and My Brain Working)

Alright, another week, another check-in on the side projects. It's funny how these little personal tools become so ingrained in my workflow, even when they're just chugging along in the background. This past period was mostly about maintenance and ensuring the data keeps flowing into my personal knowledge bases.

**What are these things anyway?**

First up, `halachot-orvishua`. This is a super niche project for me, basically a personal scraper and aggregator for specific religious content from a trusted online source. The goal is to pull in Q&A and articles, make them searchable, and generally just have a personal, curated library of information that's important to me. It's about building my own digital library, one piece of content at a time.

Then there's the `RAG Knowledge Base Pipeline`. This is the more ambitious sibling. It takes the idea of a personal knowledge base and cranks it up with Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG). Think of it as a system that ingests various types of content – blog posts, comments, notes – and then makes them queryable in a really smart way using LLMs. It's my attempt to build a "second brain" that I can actually talk to, getting insights from my own data.

**What actually happened this week?**

For `halachot-orvishua`, it was a pretty quiet week, which is actually a good thing. The sync ran as scheduled, and it picked up `1 new Q&A`. No new articles this time, but that one Q&A means the system is still alive, still checking, and still adding value. It's a testament to the "set it and forget it" ideal, mostly.

The `RAG Knowledge Base Pipeline` also had a couple of syncs. On June 8th and again on June 11th, the pipeline ran. Both times, it didn't find any *new* posts, but it did detect and process `1 comment update` each time. This is actually pretty cool and important. It means the system isn't just looking for brand new content; it's smart enough to detect changes within existing documents, specifically comments. This is crucial for keeping the RAG up-to-date without having to re-ingest entire articles every time someone fixes a typo or adds a follow-up comment.

**The "Aha!" moments and the "Ugh, really?" parts**

Honestly, the biggest "aha!" this week was just the quiet satisfaction of seeing these things *work*. There weren't any major bugs or fires to put out, which is a win in itself for automated systems. The fact that the RAG pipeline is successfully detecting *comment updates* rather than just new posts was a subtle but important validation. It means the change detection logic I put in place is robust enough to handle granular updates, which was definitely a tricky part to get right initially. It's easy to just pull new stuff, but tracking changes to existing content without blowing up your vector database or re-processing everything unnecessarily is a different beast.

The "ugh, really?" moment, if I had to pick one, was just the sheer volume of *nothing* new sometimes. You build these elaborate pipelines, and then they just report "0 new posts." It's a bit anticlimactic, but then you remember that's exactly what they're *supposed* to do when there's no new content. It's a good reminder that success isn't always about flashy new features; sometimes it's just about reliable, boring execution.

**Personal Reflections**

It feels good to have these systems humming along. It's a constant reminder of the power of automation and how building tools for yourself can genuinely make your life easier and your learning more efficient. The `halachot-orvishua` project is a quiet, personal win – just knowing that specific knowledge is being archived for me is comforting.

The RAG pipeline, though, is where my head is really at. It's pushing me into the world of LLMs and practical AI applications, which is a huge learning curve but incredibly rewarding. Seeing it handle updates gracefully makes me feel like I'm actually building something robust, not just a fragile script. It's a small step, but it reinforces the idea that consistent, incremental work on these personal projects really does add up. It's about building infrastructure for my own brain, and that's a pretty cool feeling.

**What's next**

Keep monitoring the syncs and start exploring new data sources for the RAG pipeline.
